## 4. Firebase / Firestore Limits & Usage

### Firestore Free Tier (Spark Plan)
| Quota | Limit | This Project's Usage |
|---|---|---|
| **Reads / day** | 50,000 | ✅ Very low — `onSnapshot` counts 1 read per document on first sync + 1 per changed doc. 200 customers × 1 = ~200 reads/day for deliveries |
| **Writes / day** | 20,000 | ✅ Low — each status update by agent = 1 write; 200 deliveries/day ≈ 200–600 writes |
| **Deletes / day** | 20,000 | ✅ Only on reset day — batch delete of today's deliveries |
| **Storage** | 1 GB | ✅ Deliveries are cleared daily → Firestore never grows. Only customers + agents stay permanently |
| **Network egress** | 10 GB/month | ✅ Admin is 1 user; all data is minimal JSON |

### What Was Previously Causing 300,000 Reads (Now Fixed)
The old `loadHistoryCache()` called `collectionGroup('deliveries').get()` on every dashboard load — fetching ALL historical deliveries from ALL sub-collection days with no filter. This was removed. Historical data is now fetched exclusively from Supabase.

### Firestore Real-Time Listener Pattern
```
db.collection('deliveries').where('deliveryDate', '==', today).onSnapshot(...)
```
- Reads once on attach (N documents = N reads, where N = today's delivery count)
- After that: **only changed documents** trigger reads (typically 1 read per agent status update)
- Costs ~200–500 reads on login, then ~2–5 reads per minute during active delivery hours

### Firebase Auth (Free Tier)
| Limit | Value |
|---|---|
| Monthly active users | 10,000 free |
| Phone auth | Not used |
| Email/password | ✅ Used for admin + agents |

> **Agent Auth Note:** Agents are created via REST (`identitytoolkit.googleapis.com/signUp`) not the Admin SDK. This means agents are real Firebase Auth users and can have their password reset via the Firebase console.

